(project borge-db
 (doc "Database schema DSL — an extension of the borge language.
       Describes tables, columns, types, relations, CRUD operations,
       and row-level security in sexp. Generates SQL migrations and
       application-level query code via LLM. Postgres-first.
       All existing borge commands work on db sections.")
 (no-inline)
 (status planned)

 (section relationship-to-borge
  (doc "DB is an extension of the borge language, not a separate language")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Borge DB is NOT a new language. It is a set of new sexp forms
     that live inside existing .borg files. Every borge command that
     works today works on DB sections without changes:

     - borge balance: paren-checks DB sections like any other
     - borge parse: parses DB forms as sexp like anything else
     - borge fmt: formats DB sections with canonical indentation
     - borge check: validates DB section files exist and are well-formed
     - borge lint: validates DB semantics (and gains DB-aware checks)
     - borge report: counts DB section statuses like any other section
     - borge drift: detects drift between DB spec and actual schema
     - borge nodes: dumps DB AST like any other section
     - borge generate db: NEW subcommand — generates SQL from DB spec

     The principle: borge DB sections are first-class borge citizens.
     They get the same tooling, the same workflow, and the same
     spec-is-source-of-truth discipline as every other section.

     What IS new:
       1. New sexp forms: (db), (table), (column), (type),
          (references), (operations), (crud), (query), (relation),
          (join), (groups), (group), (can), (ownership)
       2. New subcommand: borge generate db
       3. Integration with borge-ui: pages can reference (data ...)
          to declare what queries they need
   |))
  )
 )

 (section philosophy
  (doc (|
    Borge DB describes database intent, not SQL. SQL is the
    implementation — it's the how. The spec is the what:
    what tables exist, what columns they have, what operations
    are available, who can access what.

    Three core principles:

    1. Schema is spec, SQL is derived. The .borg file describes
       the schema. The generator produces SQL migrations from it.
       If the generated SQL doesn't match reality, fix the spec
       or regenerate. Same discipline as borge generate code.

    2. Permissions live on the model, not on the page. Row-level
       security is declared once on the table definition and
       enforced everywhere by Postgres. Pages don't filter data
       — the database does. Declare once, enforce everywhere.

    3. Postgres-first. The DSL targets PostgreSQL specifically.
       RLS policies, UUID defaults, timestamptz, and gen_random_uuid
       are first-class concerns, not afterthoughts. Other databases
       may work but Postgres is the primary target.

    Why not just use SQL? For the same reason borge exists at all:
    the spec is the source of truth. SQL is the generated output.
    The spec is higher-level intent, diffable, reviewable, and
    checkable by borge's existing tooling. SQL migrations are
    derived artifacts.
  |))
 )

 (section schema
  (doc "Table and column definitions")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Tables are declared inside a (db ...) form:

     (db
       (table users
         (column id (type uuid) (primary-key) (default gen_random_uuid))
         (column email (type text) (unique) (not-null))
         (column name (type text) (not-null))
         (column created-at (type timestamptz) (default now)))

       (table posts
         (column id (type uuid) (primary-key) (default gen_random_uuid))
         (column author-id (type uuid) (references users.id))
         (column title (type text) (not-null))
         (column body (type text))
         (column published (type boolean) (default false))
         (column created-at (type timestamptz) (default now))))

     Table names are symbols. Column definitions contain:
       - (type NAME): the Postgres type — uuid, text, int, boolean,
         timestamptz, jsonb, etc.
       - (primary-key): this column is the primary key
       - (unique): this column has a unique constraint
       - (not-null): this column cannot be null
       - (default EXPR): default value expression — gen_random_uuid,
         now, false, true, or any SQL expression
       - (references TABLE.COLUMN): foreign key constraint

     Multiple columns can share a primary key:
       (column org-id (type uuid) (primary-key))
       (column user-id (type uuid) (primary-key))

     The generator produces CREATE TABLE statements with all
     constraints, defaults, and foreign keys.
   |))
  )

  (subsection column-types
   (doc "Supported PostgreSQL column types")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      The (type ...) form accepts PostgreSQL type names directly:

      Common types:
        uuid, text, varchar, int, bigint, boolean,
        timestamptz, date, jsonb, bytea, decimal, float

      Array types:
        (type (array text))    — text[]
        (type (array uuid))    — uuid[]

      The DSL does not abstract over types — it uses Postgres
      type names verbatim. This is opinionated but honest.
      If you're targeting Postgres, use Postgres types.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection indexes
   (doc "Index declarations for performance")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Indexes are declared on tables:

      (table users
        (column id (type uuid) (primary-key))
        (column email (type text) (unique) (not-null))
        (column name (type text) (not-null))
        (index email-idx (columns email))
        (index name-idx (columns name) (method gin)))

      (index name (columns col1 col2) (method name) (unique))
        - name: symbol identifying the index
        - columns: one or more columns to index
        - method: btree (default), gin, gist, hash, spgist, brin
        - unique: whether the index has a uniqueness constraint

      The generator produces CREATE INDEX statements.
    |))
   )
  )
 )

 (section operations
  (doc "What you can do with the data — CRUD and queries")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Operations declare what actions are available on a table:

     (operations users
       (crud)                         ;; full CRUD: create, read, update, delete
       (query by-email)               ;; lookup by email
       (query recent (limit 10)))     ;; recent users

     (operations posts
       (crud (except delete))          ;; everything except delete
       (query by-author)               ;; posts by a specific author
       (query published-recent (limit 20))) ;; recent published posts

     (crud) declares standard operations:
       - (crud): create, read, update, delete — all five
       - (crud (except delete)): all except delete
       - (crud (except create delete)): only read and update
       - (crud (only read)): read-only

     (query name) declares a named query:
       - name: symbol identifying the query
       - (limit N): optional result limit
       - The query details (WHERE clause, JOINs, etc.) are
         determined by the generator from context and naming
         conventions.

     Operations are separate from the table schema because:
       - Not every table needs CRUD (e.g., join tables)
       - Not every table allows delete
       - Queries are application-specific, not schema-level
       - The generator produces route handlers from operations
   |))
  )
 )

 (section relations
  (doc "Joins and cross-table queries")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Relations describe queries that span multiple tables:

     (relation user-posts
       (from users)
       (join posts (on posts.author-id = users.id))
       (select users.name posts.title posts.published)
       (query recent-posts-with-author))

     (relation published-with-author
       (from posts)
       (join users (on users.id = posts.author-id))
       (where (posts.published = true))
       (select users.name posts.title posts.created-at)
       (query feed (limit 20)))

     Parts:
       - (from TABLE): the primary table
       - (join TABLE (on CONDITION)): inner join
       - (left-join TABLE (on CONDITION)): left join
       - (where CONDITION): filter condition
       - (select COL1 COL2 ...): columns to return
       - (query NAME (limit N)): named query from this relation

     The generator produces SQL views or application-level queries
     from relations. For simple cases, a view; for complex cases,
     a parameterized query function.

     Relations integrate with the UI: a page can declare
     (data (relation-name query-name)) to indicate it uses
     a specific relation's query.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section ownership
  (doc "Row-level ownership — who owns a row")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     (ownership column-name) declares which column determines
     who owns a row. This is the most common RLS pattern:

     (table posts
       (column id (type uuid) (primary-key))
       (column author-id (type uuid) (references users.id))
       (column title (type text) (not-null))
       (column body (type text))
       (column published (type boolean) (default false))
       (ownership author-id))

     (ownership author-id) means:
       - Users own rows where author-id matches their identity
       - The generator produces RLS policies:
         - SELECT: users can read their own rows (and published ones,
           if a group rule says so)
         - INSERT: users can insert rows with their own author-id
         - UPDATE: users can update their own rows
         - DELETE: users can delete their own rows

     Ownership is a shorthand. Under the hood, it's equivalent
     to declaring a group with (can posts (all) (where author-id
     = current-user)). The shorthand exists because "author owns
     their own rows" is by far the most common pattern.

     For more complex ownership models, use the groups system
     directly and skip (ownership ...).
   |))
  )
 )

 (section groups
  (doc "Access control groups and their capabilities")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Groups define who can do what, at the row level. The generator
     produces Postgres RLS policies from group declarations.

     (groups
       (group admin
         (can-all))                        ;; admins can do everything
       (group author
         (can posts (create read update) (where author-id = current-user))
         (can users (read (where id = current-user))))
       (group reader
         (can posts (read (where published = true)))
         (can users (read))))

     (group name ...) declares a named group:
       - name: symbol identifying the group
       - Body: one or more (can ...) declarations

     (can-all): shorthand for all operations on all tables.
     Equivalent to bypasses RLS. Used for admin/superuser roles.

     (can TABLE OPERATIONS (where CONDITION)):
       - TABLE: which table this rule applies to
       - OPERATIONS: create, read, update, delete, or combinations
         - (read): can only read
         - (create read): can create and read
         - (create read update delete): full CRUD
       - (where CONDITION): row-level filter
         - (where author-id = current-user): column matches current user
         - (where published = true): column matches a value
         - (where id = current-user): specific row match

     The generator translates groups into RLS policies:

       (group admin (can-all))
         → ALTER TABLE ... ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
           CREATE POLICY admin_all ON ... FOR ALL TO admin
             USING (true) WITH CHECK (true);

       (group author (can posts (create read update) (where author-id = current-user)))
         → CREATE POLICY author_posts ON posts FOR SELECT TO author
             USING (author_id = current_user_id);
           CREATE POLICY author_posts_insert ON posts FOR INSERT TO author
             WITH CHECK (author_id = current_user_id);
           ...

       (group reader (can posts (read (where published = true))))
         → CREATE POLICY reader_posts ON posts FOR SELECT TO reader
             USING (published = true);

     The (current-user) expression in (where ...) is resolved
     by the generator based on the convention. For Dream/wyo.auth,
     it becomes the session user ID. The mapping is convention-specific.
   |))
  )

  (subsection group-resolution
   (doc "How group membership is determined at runtime")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      The spec declares groups declaratively. How a user is assigned
      to a group at runtime is convention-specific:

      - wyo.auth convention: group is stored as a column on the
        users table, resolved via the Dream session
      - JWT convention: group is a claim in the token
      - Application convention: group is resolved by a lookup
        query at request time

      The generator produces the right resolution code based on
      the convention. The spec just says "these groups exist and
      these are their capabilities." The convention says "here's
      how you know which group a request belongs to."

      A common pattern: the users table has a (column role (type text))
      that stores the group name. The generator produces a session
      middleware that sets the Postgres role per request.
    |))
   )
  )
 )

 (section data-integration
  (doc "How pages reference database queries")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     Pages in borge-ui can declare what data they need using
     the (data ...) form:

     (page dashboard
       (data (posts published-recent))
       (use-layout sidebar-shell)
       (fill content
         ...))

     (page profile
       (data (users by-id))
       (data (posts by-author))
       (use-layout app-shell)
       ...)

     (data (TABLE QUERY)) means: this page loads the named query
     on the specified table. The generator produces:
       - A query function or ORM call in the page handler
       - The data is available to the page template
       - RLS automatically filters results based on the user's group

     The page doesn't declare permissions — the schema and groups
     handle that. The page just says "I need this data." Postgres
     RLS ensures the user only sees what they're allowed to see.

     For relations:

       (page feed
         (data (published-with-author feed))
         (use-layout app-shell)
         ...)

     This loads the "feed" query from the "published-with-author"
     relation.
   |))
  )
 )

 (section full-example
  (doc "A complete example: blog application database")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     (db blog
       (table users
         (column id (type uuid) (primary-key) (default gen_random_uuid))
         (column email (type text) (unique) (not-null))
         (column name (type text) (not-null))
         (column role (type text) (not-null) (default "reader"))
         (column created-at (type timestamptz) (default now))
         (index email-idx (columns email)))

       (table posts
         (column id (type uuid) (primary-key) (default gen_random_uuid))
         (column author-id (type uuid) (references users.id) (not-null))
         (column title (type text) (not-null))
         (column body (type text))
         (column published (type boolean) (default false))
         (column created-at (type timestamptz) (default now))
         (column updated-at (type timestamptz))
         (ownership author-id)
         (index author-idx (columns author-id))
         (index published-idx (columns published created-at)))

       (table comments
         (column id (type uuid) (primary-key) (default gen_random_uuid))
         (column post-id (type uuid) (references posts.id) (not-null))
         (column author-id (type uuid) (references users.id) (not-null))
         (column body (type text) (not-null))
         (column created-at (type timestamptz) (default now))
         (ownership author-id)
         (index post-idx (columns post-id)))

       (operations users
         (crud (except delete))
         (query by-email)
         (query by-id))

       (operations posts
         (crud (except delete))
         (query by-author)
         (query published-recent (limit 20))
         (query by-id))

       (operations comments
         (crud (except delete))
         (query by-post))

       (relation post-with-author
         (from posts)
         (join users (on users.id = posts.author-id))
         (select users.name posts.title posts.body posts.published posts.created-at)
         (query detail))

       (relation feed-with-authors
         (from posts)
         (join users (on users.id = posts.author-id))
         (where (posts.published = true))
         (select users.name posts.title posts.created-at)
         (query feed (limit 20)))

       (groups
         (group admin
           (can-all))
         (group author
           (can posts (create read update) (where author-id = current-user))
           (can comments (create read update) (where author-id = current-user))
           (can users (read (where id = current-user))))
         (group reader
           (can posts (read (where published = true)))
           (can comments (read))
           (can users (read)))))
   |))
  )
 )

 (section convention-targets
  (doc "How the DSL maps to different targets")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     The generator reads the (db ...) spec and a target convention,
     then produces code. Each convention knows how to translate:

     postgres-sql target:
       (table ...)  → CREATE TABLE with constraints, defaults, indexes
       (crud)       → no code (RLS handles it)
       (query ...)  → named SQL function or view
       (relation ...) → CREATE VIEW or SQL function
       (ownership ...) → RLS policy using column = current_user_id
       (groups ...) → RLS policies per group

     ocaml-dream target:
       (table ...)  → SQL migration file in migrations/
       (crud)       → route handlers: GET/POST/PUT/DELETE
       (query ...)  → Dream route handler with parameterized query
       (relation ...) → Dream route handler with join query
       (ownership ...) → RLS migration + session role setting
       (groups ...) → RLS migration + role middleware

     The convention handles:
       - How current-user is resolved (session, JWT, etc.)
       - Migration file naming (sequential, timestamped)
       - SQL dialect specifics (Postgres version, extensions)
       - Connection pool and transaction management
   |))
  )
 )

 (section generation-workflow
  (doc "How borge generate produces database code from specs")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     borge generate db SECTION-NAME

     1. Read the (db ...) spec from the section
     2. Read the project convention to determine the target
     3. Assemble prompt: spec text + convention rules + existing
        migrations
     4. Call the LLM to generate SQL migrations and/or application code
     5. Write generated files
     6. Mark section (status implemented)

     Migration files are versioned. The generator produces a new
     migration for each schema change. Existing migrations are
     never modified — only new ones are added.

     Like borge generate code, the spec is always right. If the
     generated SQL doesn't match, fix the spec or regenerate.

     Drift detection: borge drift can check whether the actual
     database schema matches the spec by comparing against the
     migration history or by introspecting the live database.
   |))
 )
 )

 (section future-work
  (doc "Possible extensibility for the DB DSL")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
     - Enums: (enum status (draft published archived))
     - Triggers: (trigger update-timestamp (on update) (set updated-at = now))
     - Checks: (check positive-price (column price > 0))
     - Composite types and domains
     - Many-to-many join tables: (join-table user-roles users roles)
     - Soft delete: (soft-delete) instead of physical delete
     - Audit logging: (audit) tracks changes on a table
     - Database functions and stored procedures
     - Schema namespacing (multiple schemas in one Postgres database)
     - Partial indexes with WHERE conditions
     - Database-level constraints (exclusion constraints)
   |))
  )
 )
)
